THE $40 TRILLION LECTURE: AMERICA DISCOVERS BRITAIN HAS DISABLED PEOPLE

An urgent lesson in financial restraint from across the Atlantic. Please have your mobility allowance ready for inspection.

America’s financial press has uncovered a disturbing development.

Britain has sick people. Some receive financial support. A number have apparently used that support to leave the house.

In cars.

The Wall Street Journal’s “The Welfare State Is Crushing Britain” presents rising sickness and disability spending as a threat to the country’s financial stability. 

The warning arrives from a country whose gross federal debt passed $40 trillion in August 2026. 

An impressive vantage point from which to examine somebody else’s parking space.

But the most revealing feature of this debate is the speed at which an argument about public expenditure becomes an inspection of disabled people’s lifestyles. Suddenly, the relevant questions concern what they drive, whether they look sufficiently unwell and whether their lives contain anything that might be mistaken for enjoyment.

The balance sheet has become a character assessment.

And the defendant has arrived in a hatchback.

THE GREAT BRITISH CAR SCANDAL

Consider the much-circulated statistic that only around 10% of Motability vehicles have disability adaptations. The Motability Foundation has addressed precisely this point: around 40% of Scheme customers use a wheelchair some or all of the time, but many do not require adaptations to drive, or travel as passengers. 

The distinction should not require an international investigation.

Difficulty walking does not automatically imply difficulty sitting in an ordinary passenger seat. A carer driving a disabled person may have no need for modified controls. The Foundation’s explanation explicitly describes these different needs. 

Yet an unmodified car is offered up as though it were an unmodified medical record.

Apparently, disability must now be visible not merely in the person, but in the upholstery.

A steering wheel, unfortunately, is not a diagnostic instrument. Neither is a photograph of a Ford.

There is also a small accounting detail worth keeping in view: Motability customers use their qualifying mobility allowance to lease a vehicle. The lease payments come from that allowance, rather than arriving as an unrelated gift on top of it. 

The money funding the lease cannot simultaneously be counted as cash in the claimant’s pocket.

Even during a moral panic, money should only appear once.

A BRIEF INTERRUPTION FOR THE ACTUAL NUMBERS

The article’s roughly £2,000-a-month example combines several benefits, including housing support and PIP. It is not a £2,000 PIP payment. 

For 2026/27, the highest PIP award consists of £114.60 a week for daily living and £80 for mobility: a maximum of £194.60 weekly. That is £778.40 every four weeks, or approximately £843 per calendar month when averaged over a 52-week year. 

Those distinctions matter. Housing support, ordinary living costs and the additional costs associated with disability are different items. Adding the payments together does not make the costs they address disappear.

A rent contribution does not become spending money because a columnist has put it in the same paragraph as a car.

PIP also supports people in work. Its purpose is to help with additional living costs arising from a long-term condition or disability, assessed through difficulties with everyday activities and mobility. Employment does not automatically remove eligibility. 

Any comparison with wages therefore needs to account for both entitlements and costs on each side.

This is a financial newspaper. Expecting the categories to survive the journey into the headline hardly seems unreasonable.

FISCAL DISCIPLINE, WITH AIR SUPPORT

Now turn the microscope around.

According to the Stockholm International Peace Research Institute, the United States spent $954 billion on its military in 2025—approximately one-third of worldwide military expenditure and about 2.8 times China’s spending. 

That is almost a trillion dollars in a single year.

Watch how the vocabulary changes.

A disabled person’s transport becomes dependency. Military transport becomes capability.

A mobility allowance invites an inquiry into whether its recipient really deserves it. A weapons programme invites a presentation about strategic requirements.

Both involve public money. But only one beneficiary is expected to establish financial responsibility by having a visibly miserable life.

Perhaps Motability’s public-relations mistake was putting cup holders in the vehicles rather than missile launchers.

A Strategic Civilian Transport Readiness Programme sounds much more respectable. The same person could make the same journey in the same car, but the expenditure would at least have an acronym suitable for a procurement committee.

THE OTHER MOBILITY SCHEME

The longer financial record is more substantial still.

Brown University’s Costs of War project estimated in 2021 that the United States’ post-9/11 war-related costs and future obligations totalled approximately $8 trillion. That estimate included future veterans’ care, alongside spending already incurred or requested, but excluded future interest on war borrowing. 

Separate research estimated 38 million people displaced across eight post-9/11 wars involving the United States: Afghanistan, Iraq, Pakistan, Syria, Libya, Yemen, Somalia and the Philippines. That figure covers displacement across those conflicts, involving multiple armed actors. 

These are substantial entries in any assessment of spending priorities and human consequences.

They also provide a somewhat uncomfortable backdrop to the spectacle of British disability transport being treated as an international lesson in extravagance.

War produces costs that persist for decades. Displacement means homes, livelihoods and communities lost. A serious assessment must keep those consequences in the picture alongside the expenditure.

Yet somehow, the family hatchback makes such an inviting exhibit.

It is easier to photograph. The numbers fit neatly into a paragraph. And the person using it is considerably easier to summon for questioning than an entire foreign-policy establishment.

EXTRAORDINARY WEALTH, WITH PEOPLE SLEEPING OUTSIDE

America is exceptionally wealthy.

The UBS Global Wealth Report 2026 places 35.7% of the personal wealth in its sample in the United States. That sample covers 56 markets estimated to represent more than 92% of the world’s wealth. 

There is no shortage of financial achievement to discuss.

There is also the question of who gets to experience it.

The US Census Bureau counted 34.5 million people below the official poverty threshold in 2025, equivalent to 10.2% of the population. 

The Department of Housing and Urban Development counted 745,652 people experiencing homelessness on a single night in January 2025, including 266,320 living without shelter. 

A country can accumulate an extraordinary concentration of personal wealth while leaving hundreds of thousands of people without a permanent home. Those facts sit beside one another in the official record.

At street level, national prosperity appears to have an eligibility problem of its own.

But please, let us return to the genuinely alarming question: why does that disabled British person have somewhere comfortable to sit on the way to an appointment?

THE REAL AUDIT

The revealing question is whose spending gets treated as evidence of a moral defect.

The recipient of a procurement contract is supplying a capability. The recipient of a disability payment is invited to justify their existence, their shopping, their transport and the occasional appearance of happiness.

That is a peculiar way to conduct an audit.

A proper assessment of public spending asks what it achieves. When support enables somebody to attend an appointment, remain independent or get to work, that outcome belongs in the assessment too.

You cannot place every cost in the economic analysis and dismiss every benefit as sentimentality.

Nor does a vehicle cease to meet a disability-related need because it looks like a vehicle an able-bodied person might also choose. Public support should not come with a requirement to purchase the most humiliating version of whatever works.

The international lecture would be more impressive if its moral intensity survived the journey from mobility allowances to military appropriations—from the individual claimant to the institutions that command vastly greater sums.

Until then, perhaps the answer is branding.

Paint the Motability fleet in camouflage. Rename hospital appointments deployments. Describe getting somebody to the supermarket as maintaining a domestic supply line.

Then the expenditure might finally sound serious enough to deserve a straight face.

Because fiscal responsibility is a curious doctrine when it can discuss nearly a trillion dollars of military spending in the language of strategy, then encounter a disabled person in a Ford and demand an explanation.
